  "glossaryTerms": [],
  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Laid work covers the ground fabric with long stitches that go from one edge of the shape to the other without turning back on themselves. Unlike satin stitch, the stitches do not overlap; they sit side by side to cover the ground. A second layer of stitches then crosses the first at an angle and is tied down, adding texture and colour.",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "What you need",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "suppliesCard",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "label": "Thread",
              "value": "Stranded cotton, 3 strands for the first layer; 2 strands for the second layer"
            },
            {
              "label": "Fabric",
              "value": "Firmly woven cotton or linen"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needle",
              "value": "Embroidery needle size 9"
            },
            {
              "label": "Hoop",
              "value": "15 cm embroidery hoop"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Filling a leaf",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Start at the widest point. Bring the needle up on the left edge of the shape and insert on the right edge. The stitch lies straight across the full width.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Come up on the left edge just below the first stitch and cross to the right. Continue until the shape is covered.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Add the second layer at 45 degrees across the first, spacing stitches evenly.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "At each intersection, add a small tying stitch to hold both layers flat.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Common mistakes",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "intro": "Two common problems.",
          "items": [
            {
              "fix": "Keep each stitch immediately next to the previous one. They should touch but not overlap.",
              "cause": "Stitches placed too far apart.",
              "symptom": "Gaps appear between the first-layer stitches"
            },
            {
              "fix": "Keep the first layer at relaxed tension. The second layer and tying stitches will hold the shape flat.",
              "cause": "Too much thread tension on long stitches.",
              "symptom": "The surface buckles"
            }
          ],
          "heading": "When laid work goes wrong"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Uses and related stitches"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Laid work is a core filling in Jacobean embroidery, where every "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "leaf",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "techniqueLink",
                "attrs": {
                  "techniqueSlug": "embroidery-leaf-stitch",
                  "label": "leaf"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " or petal is filled with a different laid pattern. It uses thread more efficiently than "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "satin",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "techniqueLink",
                "attrs": {
                  "techniqueSlug": "embroidery-satin-stitch",
                  "label": "satin"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " and is suitable for very large shapes where satin would be too heavy or costly."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}